 A piranha or piraa (/ p r n j /, / p r n j /, or / p r n /; Portuguese: [pi], Spanish: ), a member of family Serrasalmidae, or a member of the subfamily Serrasalminae within the tetra family, Characidae in order Characiformes, is a freshwater fish that inhabits South American rivers, floodplains, lakes and reservoirs.. Report a sighting. Although they have a reputation of being very aggressive, they are very sociable fish living in schools in the wild. Red-bellied piranhas have a lifespan of 10 years or more. Location in the Zoo. Cookie Consent plugin for the EU cookie law 9 comments. As their name suggests, they have a reddish stomach with females having a deeper red color. The red-bellied piranha is considered one of the more dangerous and aggressive species of . They vary in color by location, population, and age, but many are silvery-gray or golden and some have bright Temperament: Community. The fish glides closer and opens its mouth to reveal rows of razor-sharp, triangular teeth. The species within the Pygocentrus species, a genus of the Piranha family that includes the famous Red-Bellied Piranha are a schooling fish, meaning they will swimaround together. Cool Animal Fact. Preserve (freeze) the specimen and immediately contact the CDFW Invasive Species Program, or email us at firstname.lastname@example.org, or (866) 440-9530. The red-bellied piranha or red piranha (Pygocentrus nattereri) is a species of piranha native to South America, found in the Amazon River Basin, . The eggs are laid in the sand, below the water source in which they live that has been dug by her mate. The Red-Bellied Piranha ( Pygocentrus nattereri) is the most dangerous type of Piranha. Gene provides a unified query environment for genes defined by sequence and/or in NCBI's Map Viewer. Close-up photos, selective focus. The older the fish, the darker it . He can't form a new pair bond until then! . The Red Bellied Piranha (Pygocentrus nattereri) is a species of Piranha from the family C