yeah, thinking time… good thinking! I forget the importance of this, probably as I get a bit scared of silence in the classroom. I guess this comes with confidence though, and realising that silence may not actually be a bad thing… Cheers for the tips as always. By the way your new site layout looks very snazzy!
I know what you mean and, believe me, it was quite awkward at first. My students didn’t seem to see any point in keeping quiet and thinking. I decided to be consistent and it worked (with some groups better than with others, of course). Try it and don’t get discouraged by some initial resistance/questioning the point of it. PS: the layout was a very belated birthday gift, no way I would be able to pull it off!
These are great! I also use Classdojo (www.classdojo.com) to encourage speaking, classroom participation, interaction and to discourage L1 chat, but you need a computer or device kids can see.
My (mostly Italian) students are not as quiet in class as I think other cultures can be, but they always need to have a genuine reason for speaking to each other and they need the language to do it confidently – no one (but especially not teens) wants to be tongue-tied in front of classmates!
Great tips, thanks!
